From b2d88fa012195f4b01f380d5172a3a9738881ac9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grizbeard <justin.morken@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 15:56:49 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 07/11] Draw vertical waveforms the way <Orientation> already
 claims

<Orientation>vertical</Orientation> on a <Visual> has parsed and reached
WaveformWidgetRenderer for a long time, but the allshader backend never
acted on it: rendergraph::Engine::resize carried a commented-out TODO
where the quarter turn belongs, and matrixForWidgetGeometry(), the helper
written for exactly this, is referenced by nothing but its own header.
What you got was a renderer that thought the time axis was the widget's
height while the painter still drew along its width, so the signal was
squeezed into `height` pixels of `width` and sat off its axis.

Every renderer already builds its geometry in (length, breadth) space, so
turning the scene is all the signal, the beat grid, the pre-roll and the
mark lines need. Engine::resize takes a quarterTurn flag, applies the
rotation the TODO described, and hands nodes the turned extent.

Two things the turn alone does not cover:

The end-of-track flash sized itself from the widget's width and height
rather than length and breadth, so its gradient ran across the track
instead of along it.

Mark labels need the opposite treatment. The image has to stay unturned
or the mark's *line* stops spanning the breadth -- turning it undoes
exactly what the scene turn achieved -- so only the chip inside it is
built on its side, and it comes out upright once the scene turns.

Not covered: the "time until next mark" digits overlay, which is off by
default and still renders on its side, and the legacy non-allshader
renderers, which are left as they were.

Verified on a 1920x480 skin with both the RGB and Filtered waveform
types: time runs down the lane, the beat grid is horizontal, the playhead
is a horizontal line, and cue and hotcue chips read upright.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
